<page "tree usage" notebook>
	<header level="1">
		Tree
		<header>A tree shows rows nested in rows, which open and close, and can be selected one or many at a time.</>
	</>
	<section>
		<toolbar class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write your rows as children, each row holding the rows inside it.
			- A row says `label`, and `which` one, quieter, with an `icon`.
			- `open` shows the rows inside it.  A row whose rows are drawn only once it opens says `expandable`.
			- `ui-changed` hands over what's selected.  To decide that yourself, cancel `ui-selecting`, and set each row's `selected`.
			- `filter` looks only at rows that are drawn.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("ui-tree").addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => show(event.detail.values))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			The tree is one tab stop.
			- Up and down move between the rows on screen;  Home and End go to the ends.
			- Right opens a row, then goes into it;  left closes it, then goes out to its parent.
			- Enter and Space select, with Shift, Command or Control as a click takes them.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>It's a `tree` of `treeitem`s, each saying its level, and whether it's open and selected.  Give it a `label`.</>
	</>
</page>
